# Wrinkles and detail
Source and migration plan: `maya_tools/docs/rig_performance_plan.md`.
- Define Animation, Review and Final/Export evaluation contracts using the same
  controls and animation. Specify visible output, enabled work and consumers.
- Animation must bypass expensive wrinkle/detail branches, not just hide output
  or multiply displacement by zero. Prove skipped work using Profiler and active
  graph/consumer checks. Connected or animated visibility can prevent pruning.
- `rig_performance.py` supports explicit primary/detail mesh routing. Install it
  only on owned wrinkle outputs and verify skipped compute. Final geometry must
  have visible ancestors; hidden utility groups invalidate display benchmarks.
- Drive garments and attachments from validated low/primary proxy geometry in
  Animation. Route every consumer before freezing/bypassing a high branch; never
  freeze a source that an active garment, pin, export or simulation needs.
- Do not change connections, build nodes or invalidate the Evaluation Graph every
  frame. Mode changes are explicit, reversible transactions at workflow boundaries.
- Wrinkle rest neighbors, edge lengths, flow maps, masks and topology signatures
  are computed/stored once. Recompute only after a validated model/rest change.
- Keep compression/displacement local-space consistent and independent of rigid
  motion. Test global/nonuniform scale so scaling does not create false wrinkles.
- Restrict runtime work to active regions where practical. Precompute sparse
  supports and shared fields; validate smoothing halos and region boundaries.
  A zero dense mask does not prove that its computation was skipped.
- Store compact runtime arrays and full editing fields separately. Encode zero
  indices so native Maya serialization preserves them; test reopen with active
  wrinkles, not only a neutral result. Keep one displayed head mesh.
- Reuse normals only when their deformation stage is correct. Profile the input
  and output normal passes before removal; displacement needs posed normals and
  the displayed result must retain correct shading.
- Skin/Bifrost/Maya conversion is a separate boundary. Measure transfers and
  residency; the output does not inherit upstream GPU eligibility.
- Put silhouette-changing folds in geometric detail. Consider shader normal/bump
  or displacement maps for microdetail, with camera/render/contact validation.
  Shader microdetail cannot replace silhouette, export or collision geometry.
- Prototype native blendShape pose wrinkles or cached final geometry when the
  measured procedural cost warrants it. Keep the editable Bifrost source and
  preset; train/fit on separate poses and validate held-out combinations.
- Stateful simulation/cloth/muscles require ordered evaluation and explicit cache
  invalidation. Keep them outside the base animation rig; validate cached delivery
  and avoid claiming random-access correctness for an uncached simulation.
- Cached speedups require an independent geometry/invalidation pass. Edward's
  current full-quality cache failed this gate; default Review/Export to uncached
  evaluation until matching evidence approves a replacement.
- Preserve presets, controls, rest fields and SKC provenance. Restore full
  quality without stale results. Skin export must expose the owned primary DAG
  branch, then restore output and garment connections.
